INT. SUBWAY CAR - MOVING - DAY

ON CAMERA: handheld, shaky. Rush hour. Bodies. Breath.

KENJI ABARA, 17, holds a camcorder at chest height. Narrating low to his lens.

KENJI
(whispering, to camera)
Day three. Same car. Same guy.

PAN to JAMAL STRICKLAND, 40s, suit, earbuds in, pressed against the doors. Not looking at anyone.

Crowd.

KENJI (CONT'D)
(still whispering)
He gets on at Fulton. Every day. Stands right there. Takes up the whole pole.

Train lurches. Squeal of brakes.

THE COMMUTERS shift as one, a wall of coats and elbows. A bag swings into Kenji.

COMMUTERS
(muttering)
Sorry. Excuse me. Mind the door.

Silence.

Kenji lowers the camera. Raises it again, slightly higher.

KENJI
(to Jamal)
Hey. Your hand's on my sister's bag.

JAMAL
(pulling an earbud, flat)
What?

KENJI
(louder)
Her bag. You've been touching it for three stops.

Nothing. The car goes quiet around them. Someone looks at their shoes.

A young woman, DEE ABARA, 15, Kenji's sister, edges forward, clutching the strap against her chest. She doesn't look up.

JAMAL
(smiling thinly, to the car)
Kid's confused. Crowded train. It happens.

Kenji's grip on the camera whitens. The light on the lens glows red.

KENJI
(very quiet)
Say it again. Into the camera.

Jamal's smile drops. He lunges for the lens, Kenji pulls back, Dee shoves between them, a briefcase hits the floor, THE COMMUTERS surge back, the pole shakes, a stranger shouts for the emergency cord.

The camera tumbles. Spins. The floor. Shoes. Black.
